    id: "f8e350401b1dc34e1aa9",
    cat: "conflitti",
    title: "Three Saudi nationals killed in Houthi attacks on Riyadh airport",
    summary: "The missile attacks come amid an escalation in fighting between the Iran-backed group and Saudi Arabia, which backs Yemen's government.",
    preview: "Three Saudi Arabian nationals have been killed in two attacks on the international airport in the capital, Riyadh, the country's civil aviation authority has said, after Yemen's Houthis said they had targeted the facility. The attacks also resulted in \"injuries to a number of Saudi citizens and residents of various nationalities\", the General Authority of Civil Aviation said in a statement on X. It added that the first attack on Thursday targeted the airport while the second had struck an aircraft belonging to state carrier Saudia.",
    body: "Three Saudi Arabian nationals have been killed in two attacks on the international airport in the capital, Riyadh, the country's civil aviation authority has said, after Yemen's Houthis said they had targeted the facility.\n\nThe attacks also resulted in \"injuries to a number of Saudi citizens and residents of various nationalities\", the General Authority of Civil Aviation said in a statement on X.\n\nIt added that the first attack on Thursday targeted the airport while the second had struck an aircraft belonging to state carrier Saudia.\n\nOne of its pilots was among those killed, the airline said in a statement.\n\nAir traffic had returned to normal, the civil aviation authority said.\n\nYemen's Houthi group said on Thursday it had attacked the airport with two ballistic missiles.\n\nThe Iran-aligned group said it had also targeted an airport in Najran city near the Saudi border with Yemen, and Khamis Mushait air base further north. Saudi Arabia has not confirmed these attacks.\n\nThe latest casualties follow the deaths of three people in Houthi attacks on two Saudi airports on 6 and 7 October.\n\nTwo were killed and 28 wounded in an attack at Abha International Airport, the civil aviation body said on Wednesday. Another person was killed at King Khalid airport in Riyadh.\n\nThe latest attacks come amid mark an escalation in renewed fighting between Yemen's Saudi-backed government forces and the Houthis, who control most of the country's north-west.\n\nThe Houthi military spokesman said the previous two attacks on Saudi Arabia's airports were in \"response to the Saudi aggression against our country\" that saw \"60 air strikes and missile attacks in the past 24 hours\".\n\nThe group says it is targeting Saudi Arabia over its support for government forces in Yemen's civil war.\n\nThe conflict has worsened in recent weeks, after the Houthis seized strategically important stretches of the Red Sea coast in a rapid offensive.\n\nThat included the port of Mokha on the Bab al-Mandab Strait, which is the narrowest point of the Red Sea and a chokepoint for global shipping.\n\nBut Yemeni pro-government forces have since said they had \"secured\" the Bab al-Mandab Strait at the southern end of the Red Sea.\n\nA Saudi-led coalition was formed in 2015 after Arab countries joined forces to stop the Houthis taking over Yemen from the country's internationally recognised government.\n\nA temporary ceasefire was brokered in 2022 after years of air strikes and ground fighting led to the deaths of hundreds of thousands of people, triggering one of the world's worst humanitarian crises.\n\nBut the informal ceasefire began to unravel in July.\n\nThe Houthis announced a \"maritime embargo\" against Saudi Arabia and began missile and drone attacks on Saudi airports, oil facilities and tankers in the Red Sea. They said they were retaliating against what they say is a years-long Saudi blockade and for an air strike on Sanaa airport.\n\nRelated topics\n\n- Published24 September",
